   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.15",
    "topic_title": "Semi-log Plots",
    "stem": "An exponential function is given by y = 16·4ˣ. A semi-log plot places x on the horizontal axis and log₄(y) on the vertical axis. What are the slope and vertical intercept of the resulting line?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Slope 4 and vertical intercept 16",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Untransformed-parameter error: this treats the exponential base and initial value as the line’s slope and intercept. On the semi-log plot, those parameters become log₄(4) and log₄(16). Fix: Take the logarithm of each parameter before identifying the slope and intercept."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Slope 1 and vertical intercept 2",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Taking log₄ of both sides gives log₄(y) = log₄(16) + x·log₄(4) = 2 + x. Thus the line has slope 1 and vertical intercept 2; the logarithm base 4 satisfies the requirement that the base exceed 1."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Slope 2 and vertical intercept 1",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Slope-intercept reversal: this swaps the constant term and the coefficient of x in log₄(y) = 2 + x. The coefficient of x is the slope, while the constant is the vertical intercept. Fix: Identify the coefficient of x first, then evaluate the vertical coordinate at x = 0."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Slope 1 and vertical intercept 0",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Initial-factor omission: this drops the contribution of 16 when taking logarithms. At x = 0, y = 16, so the plotted vertical coordinate is log₄(16) = 2, not 0. Fix: Substitute x = 0 into the original model and take log₄ of the output."
     }
    ]
   },
